# Test and verification scope

Keep Playwright and existing tests available. Do not use them as a default after every change.

This project rule overrides any general “always verify UI in the browser” habit.

## Normal code changes

- Run **one** targeted typecheck and only the **relevant** Vitest/package tests.
- Do **not** run Playwright automatically.
- Do **not** run the full E2E suite unless the task is major E2E verification or the user explicitly asks.
- Do **not** browser-verify simple copy, styling tweaks, minor layout changes, or internal refactors.
- Use Cursor Browser or Playwright only for **significant interactive** changes, **critical user flows**, or when the user **explicitly** asks for browser/E2E verification.
- Skip Cursor Browser when automated tests already cover the change.
- Never run the same typecheck or test twice.
- Avoid root `pnpm test`, `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm lint`, and Turbo verification for small scoped changes. Use the package that changed (`pnpm --filter @eveider/<pkg> exec tsc --noEmit`, `vitest run <file>`).
- Never start another Next dev server if one is already running (reuse `pnpm --filter @eveider/web-manager dev` on port 3000).
- Never seed or reset the database unless the task specifically requires it.

## Playwright (only when genuinely necessary)

Use it only when the change is a **significant interactive** change or a **critical user flow** (auth/session, protected routing, role-gated journeys) **and** E2E verification is needed — or the user asks.

From `apps/web-manager` **only**:

```
pnpm test:e2e e2e/<file>.spec.ts
```

Never add an extra `--` (that can run the full suite). Never `pnpm --filter @eveider/web-manager test:e2e -- …`.

Playwright failures must not block unrelated work.
